# 6. DETERMINISTIC CLASSIFICATION ENGINE

Create a reusable TypeScript classification service.

Suggested module structure:

```text
src/lib/classification/
  engine.ts
  rule-matcher.ts
  scoring.ts
  confidence.ts
  folder-mappings.ts
  keyword-dictionary.ts
  taxonomy.ts
  types.ts
  classification-service.ts
```

Adapt to the existing project structure.

Do not couple the classification algorithm to React components.

### Classification Inputs

The engine should use available signals:

1. Explicit user-selected category.
2. Existing manual category lock.
3. Original browser folder path.
4. Approved folder mappings.
5. Exact URL rules.
6. Hostname/domain rules.
7. URL path and query patterns.
8. Bookmark title.
9. Existing description.
10. Keyword dictionary.
11. Owner-defined rules.
12. Previously approved classification corrections.

Existing safe metadata already stored in the database may be used.

Do not implement remote website scraping or new metadata-fetching services during Phase 3.

### Classification Flow

```text
Bookmark
   |
   v
Check Manual Category / Lock
   |
   +-- Locked --> Preserve Current Category
   |
   v
Collect Classification Signals
   |
   v
Match Approved Rules
   |
   v
Match Source Folder Rules
   |
   v
Match URL / Host / Path
   |
   v
Match Title / Description / Keywords
   |
   v
Score Category Candidates
   |
   v
Apply Rule Priority and Conflict Handling
   |
   v
High Confidence?
   |
   +-- Yes --> Assign Category
   |
   +-- No --> Suggest or Add to Review Queue
   |
   v
Store Decision, Evidence & Confidence
```

The engine must return deterministic results for identical inputs and the same ruleset version.

